{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PILOT PARITY RUN (N=1) — Bedrock vs direct Anthropic, provider as the ONLY variable. Operator authorized running experiments ahead of implementation.\n\nThis is NOT the ParityReport this story's AC requires (that needs the standing eval corpus at N>=3). It is a single paired sample run to find out EARLY whether the substrate is viable at all, because if rebar's structured-output path does not survive the Converse API then S8 is blocked and the corpus run would only tell us so more expensively.\n\nPROTOCOL: the same gate operation (`rebar review-plan`) on the SAME ticket (281f-3336-c668-4979) at the SAME `--ref HEAD`, same plan text, same code tree. Only `REBAR_LLM_MODEL` differed. Account 896586841071 / us-east-1.\n\n  v1 direct Anthropic  model=claude-sonnet-4-6\n  v2 Bedrock           model=bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6\n\nRESULT — decision-level agreement is EXACT:\n\n  |                     | v1 Anthropic          | v2 Bedrock            |\n  | verdict             | PASS                  | PASS                  |\n  | blocking findings   | 0                     | 0                     |\n  | advisory findings   | 2                     | 2                     |\n  | advisory criteria   | ac-quality (P6),      | ac-quality (P6),      |\n  |                     | file-impact-cov (P9)  | file-impact-cov (P9)  |\n  | llm_calls           | 4                     | 4                     |\n  | runner              | pydantic_ai           | pydantic_ai           |\n\nSo on this sample: zero decision-level flips, and agreement holds at the finer CRITERION level too (the same two criteria fired, not merely the same counts). Criterion (a) structured-output validity HELD — the run completed through the Converse API with no StructuredOutputError, no contract violation, and the same call count, which is the specific thing this story flags as most at risk because Converse differs in structured-output handling.\n\nSECONDARY OBSERVATION — LATENCY. Bedrock was ~35% slower on model time for identical work: llm_ms 26531.6 vs 19689.9 (total_ms 27135.0 vs 20305.6), same 4 calls. One sample, no concurrency, so treat as a hint not a measurement — but the parity protocol should record latency per pass, because the gate runs on the critical path of every landing and a 35% model-time regression across every Pass-1 fan-out is a throughput question S8 has not costed.\n\nCAVEATS, stated so this is not over-read:\n- N=1, one ticket, one criterion mix, PASS/PASS. A paired sample where both sides agree on an easy verdict is the weakest possible evidence of agreement; the corpus run must include tickets that BLOCK, since a flip on a blocking verdict is the failure mode that matters.\n- Both sides ran SONNET. This says nothing about the opus path, which is where the per-pass model requirement (see eb6e) and the temperature defect (1903-868c-b704-4a80, which BLOCKS eb6e) both live. A parity run that only exercises sonnet would miss exactly the configuration Pass-1 is meant to use.\n- Caching differed in a way that is NOT a Bedrock property: v2 reported aggregate cache_write_tokens=2132 / cache_read_tokens=0, and both runs emitted spurious per-call \"caching had NO effect\" warnings on sub-4096-token calls. That warning is miscalibrated on BOTH providers — filed as 7a79-a920-afe7-40cf — so it must not be read as a Bedrock caching regression when the corpus run emits it."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785444012202005001,"uuid":"673006b4-790e-4daa-9811-fc0e5054a494"}